To Weland came woes and wearisome trial,
And cares oppressed the constant earl;
His lifelong companions were pain and sorrow, …

. . . . . . . . . . was broken;
He bade the young barons abandon their horses,
To drive them afar and dash quickly forth, …

Before thou wast born, there was built thee a house;
For thee was a mould meant ere thy mother bore thee;
They have not made it ready nor reckoned its depth; …

With my mouth I am master of many a language;
Cunningly I carol; I discourse full oft
In melodious lays; loud do I call, …

I'm found under water, held fast by my mouth;
Swirl of the sea-tides goes sweeping beneath me.
Fathom-deep sunk under surges, I grew. …

I war with the wind, with the waves I wrestle;
I must battle with both when the bottom I seek,
My strange habitation by surges o'er-roofed. …

I'm prized by men, in the meadows I'm found,
Gathered on hill-sides, and hunted in groves;
From dale and from down, by day I am brought. …

Long is the day without Usnagh's Children;
It was never mournful to be in their company.
A king's sons, by whom exiles were rewarded, …

White shields they carry in their hands,
With emblems of pale silver;
With glittering blue swords, …

I am Eve, great Adam's wife,
'Tis I that outraged Jesus of old;
'Tis I that robbed my children of Heaven, …

Summer has come, healthy and free,
Whence the brown wood is aslope;
The slender nimble deer leap, …

Four men stood by the grave of a man,
The grave of Alexander the Proud;
They sang words without falsehood …

A hedge of trees surrounds me,
A blackbird's lay sings to me;
Above my lined booklet …

At Ch’ang-an--a full foot of snow;
A levée at dawn--to bestow congratulations on the Emperor.
Just as I was nearing the Gate of the Silver Terrace, …

At the western window I paused from writing rescripts;
The pines and bamboos were all buried in stillness.
The moon rose and a calm wind came; …

We talked together in the Yung-shou Temple;
We parted to the north of the Hsin-ch’ang dyke.
Going home--I shed a few tears, …

When the yellow bird’s note was almost stopped;
And half formed the green plum’s fruit;
Sitting and grieving that spring things were over, …

Ruined and ill,--a man of two score;
  Pretty and guileless,--a girl of three.
Not a boy,--but, still better than nothing: …

Sad, sad--lean with long illness;
Monotonous, monotonous--days and nights pass.
The summer trees have clad themselves in shade; …

Deep the waters of the Black Pool, coloured like ink;
They say a Holy Dragon lives there, whom men have never seen.
Beside the Pool they have built a shrine; the authorities have …

            In the land of Tao-chou
            Many of the people are dwarfs;
The tallest of them never grow to more than three feet. …

I was going to the City to sell the herbs I had plucked;
On the way I rested by some trees at the Blue Gate.
Along the road there came a horseman riding; …

At Hsin-fēng an old man--four-score and eight;
The hair on his head and the hair of his eyebrows--white as the
    new snow. …

White billows and huge waves block the river crossing;
Wherever I go, danger and difficulty; whatever I do, failure.
Just as in my worldly career I wander and lose the road, …

I take your poems in my hand and read them beside the candle;
The poems are finished: the candle is low: dawn not yet come.
With sore eyes by the guttering candle still I sit in the dark, …

A bend of the river brings into view two triumphal arches;
That is the gate in the western wall of the suburbs of Hsün-yang.
I have still to travel in my solitary boat three or four leagues-- …

At Nine Rivers, in the tenth year, in winter,--heavy snow;
The river-water covered with ice and the forests broken with their
    load. …

Yesterday I heard that such-a-one was gone;
This morning they tell me that so-and-so is dead.
Of friends and acquaintances more than two-thirds …

My niece, who is six years old, is called “Miss Tortoise”;
My daughter of three,--little “Summer Dress.”
One is beginning to learn to joke and talk; …

Trees growing--right in front of my window;
The trees are high and the leaves grow thick.
Sad alas! the distant mountain view …

I have been ill so long that I do not count the days;
At the southern window, evening--and again evening.
Sadly chirping in the grasses under my eaves …

            I grew up at Jung-yang;
            I was still young when I left.
On and on,--forty years passed …

Our party scattered at yellow dusk and I came home to bed;
I woke at midnight and went for a walk, leaning heavily on a friend.
As I lay on my pillow my vinous complexion, soothed by sleep, grew …

All the morning I have lain perversely in bed;
Now at dusk I rise with many yawns.
My warm stove is quick to get ablaze; …

I break up cypress and make a book-box;
The box well-made,--and the cypress-wood tough.
In it shall be kept what author’s works? …

Between thirty and forty, one is distracted by the Five Lusts;
Between seventy and eighty, one is a prey to a hundred diseases.
But from fifty to sixty one is free from all ills; …

Mounting on high I begin to realize the smallness of Man’s Domain;
Gazing into distance I begin to know the vanity of the Carnal World.
I turn my head and hurry home--back to the Court and Market, …

At night you came and took my hand and we wandered together in my
    dream;
When I woke in the morning there was no one to stop the tears that …

“Those who speak know nothing;
Those who know are silent.”
These words, as I am told, …

They have put my bed beside the unpainted screen;
They have shifted my stove in front of the blue curtain.
I listen to my grandchildren, reading me a book; …

For ten years I never left my books;
I went up ... and won unmerited praise.
My high place I do not much prize; …

At dawn I rode to escort the Doctors of Art;
In the eastern quarter the sky was still grey.
I said to myself, “You have started far too soon,” …

Propped on pillows, not attending to business;
For two days I’ve lain behind locked doors.
I begin to think that those who hold office …

Tillers of the soil have few idle months;
In the fifth month their toil is double-fold.
A south-wind visits the fields at night: …

The crane from the shore standing at the top of the steps;
The moon on the pool seen at the open door;
Where these are, I made my lodging-place …

Unrewarded, my will to serve the State;
At my closed door autumn grasses grow.
What could I do to ease a rustic heart? …

Worldly matters again draw my steps;
Worldly things again seduce my heart.
Whenever for long I part from Li Chien …

The flower of the pear-tree gathers and turns to fruit;
The swallows’ eggs have hatched into young birds.
When the Seasons’ changes thus confront the mind …

I have got patronage, but am too lazy to use it;
I have got land, but am too lazy to farm it.
My house leaks; I am too lazy to mend it. …

My house is poor; those that I love have left me;
My body sick; I cannot join the feast.
There is not a living soul before my eyes …

The days of my youth left me long ago;
And now in their turn dwindle my years of prime.
With what thoughts of sadness and loneliness …

I hug my pillow and do not speak a word;
In my empty room no sound stirs.
Who knows that, all day a-bed, …

Sullen, sullen, my brows are ever knit;
Silent, silent, my lips will not move.
It is not indeed that I choose to sorrow thus; …

Washed by the rain, dust and grime are laid;
Skirting the river, the road’s course is flat.
The moon has risen on the last remnants of night; …

I dismount from my horse at the Hsi-lin Temple;
I throw the porter my slender riding-whip.
In the morning I work at a Government office-desk; …

When the sun rose I was still lying in bed;
An early oriole sang on the roof of my house.
For a moment I thought of the Royal Park at dawn …

At night I dreamt I was back in Ch‘ang-an;
I saw again the faces of old friends.
And in my dreams, under an April sky, …

My long poem, the “Eternal Grief,” is a beautiful and moving
    work;
My ten “Songs of Shensi” are models of tunefulness. …

Within the Gorges there is no lack of men;
They are people one meets, not people one cares for.
At my front door guests also arrive; …

The province I govern is humble and remote;
Yet our festivals follow the Courtly Calendar.
At rise of day we sacrificed to the Wind God, …

Heat and cold, dusk and dawn have crowded one upon the other;
Suddenly I find it is two years since I came to Chung-chou.
Through my closed doors I hear nothing but the morning and evening …

Men’s hearts love gold and jade;
Men’s mouths covet wine and flesh.
Not so the old man of the stream; …

We had rode long and were still far from the inn;
My eyes grew dim; for a moment I fell asleep.
Under my right arm the whip still dangled; …

Elders and officers line the returning road;
Wine and soup load the parting table.
I have not ruled you with the wisdom of Shao Kung; …

The early light of the rising sun shines on the beams of my house;
The first banging of opened doors echoes like the roll of a drum.
The dog lies curled on the stone step, for the earth is wet with dew; …

Around my garden the little wall is low;
In the bailiff’s lodge the lists are seldom checked.
I am ashamed to think we were not always kind; …

The western wind has blown but a few days;
Yet the first leaf already flies from the bough.
On the drying paths I walk in my thin shoes; …

At dawn I sighed to see my hairs fall;
At dusk I sighed to see my hairs fall.
For I dreaded the time when the last lock should go ... …

In an idle hour I thought of former days;
And former friends seemed to be standing in the room.
And then I wondered “Where are they now?” …

The World cheats those who cannot read;
I, happily, have mastered script and pen.
The World cheats those who hold no office; …

The town visitor’s easy talk flows in an endless stream;
The country host’s quiet thoughts ramble timidly on.
“I beg you, Sir, do not tell me about things at Ch‘ang-an; …

In length of days and soundness of limb you and I are one;
Our eyes are not wholly blind, nor our ears quite deaf.
Deep drinking we lie together, fellows of a spring day; …

Keep off your thoughts from things that are past and done;
For thinking of the past wakes regret and pain.
Keep off your thoughts from thinking what will happen; …

O my Companion, Night is passing fair,
Fairer than aught the dawn and sundown wear;
And fairer, too, than all the gilded days …

Mohammed or Messiah! Hear thou me,
The truth entire nor here nor there can be;
How should our God who made the sun and moon …

Ay, like the circles which the sun doth spin
Of gossamer, we end as we begin;
Our feet are on the heads of those that pass, …

Of Fate. Alas! and we shall follow soon
Into the night eternal or the noon;
The wayward daughters of the spheres return …

Tread lightly, for the mighty that have been
Might now be breathing in the dust unseen;
Lightly, the violets beneath thy feet …

Nor king nor slave the hungry Days will spare;
Between their fangéd Hours alike we fare:
Anon they bound upon us while we play …

Upon the threshing-floor of life I burn
Beside the Winnower a word to learn;
And only this: Man's of the soil and sun, …

Seek not the Tavern of Belief, my friend,
Until the Sakis there their morals mend;
A lie imbibed a thousand lies will breed, …

Why linger here, why turn another page?
Oh! seal with doubt the whole book of the age;
Doubt every one, even him, the seeming slave …

Of Ababil! I would the tale were true,--
Would all the birds were such winged furies too;
The scourging and the purging were a boon …

Methinks Allah divides me to complete
His problem, which with Xs is replete;
For I am free and I am too in chains …

Ah, many have I seen of those who fell
While drawing, with a swagger, from the Well;
They came with Rope and Bucket, and they went …

The I in me standing upon the brink
Would leap into the Well to get a drink;
But how to rise once in the depth, I cry, …

How like a door the knowledge we attain,
Which door is on the bourne of the Inane;
It opens and our nothingness is closed,-- …

Hither we come unknowing, hence we go;
Unknowing we are messaged to and fro;
And yet we think we know all things of earth …

The Earth then spake: "My children silent be;
Same are to God the camel and the flea:
He makes a mess of me to nourish you, …

With better skill he even will remould
The scattered potsherds of the New and Old;
Then you and I will not disdain to buy, …

I laugh, but in my laughter-cup I pour
The tears of scorn and melancholy sore;
I who am shattered by the hand of Doubt, …

The tavern and the temple also shun,
For sheikh and libertine in sooth are one;
And when the pious knave begins to pule, …

"The wine's forbidden," say these honest folk,
But for themselves the law they will revoke;
The snivelling sheikh says he's without a garb, …

The Prophets, too, among us come to teach,
Are one with those who from the pulpit preach;
They pray, and slay, and pass away, and yet …

Oh! let them in the marshes grope, or ride
Their jaded Myths along the mountain-side;
Come up with me, O Brother, to the heights …

Swathe thee in wool, my Sufi friend, and go
Thy way; in cotton I the wiser grow;
But we ourselves are shreds of earth, and soon …

Ay! suddenly the mystic Hand will seal
The saint's devotion and the sinner's weal;
They worship Saturn, but I worship One …

For all my learning's but a veil, I guess,
Veiling the phantom of my nothingness;
Howbeit, there are those who think me wise, …

Allah, my sleep is woven through, it seems,
With burning threads of night and golden beams;
But when my dreams are evil they come true; …

The subtle ways of Destiny I know;
In me she plays her game of "Give and Go."
Misfortune I receive in cash, but joy, …

A prince of souls, 'tis sung in ancient lay,
One morning sought a vesture of the clay;
He came into the Pottery, the fool-- …

But I was not. Oh! that the Fates decree
That I now cast aside this clay of me;
My soul and body wedded for a while …

Once I have tried to string a few Pearl-seeds
Upon my Rosary of wooden beads;
But I have searched, and I have searched in vain …

Thy wealth can shed no tears around thy bier,
Nor can it wash thy hands of shame and fear;
Ere thou departest with it freely part,-- …

For me thy silks and feathers have no charm
The pillow I like best is my right arm;
The comforts of this passing show I spurn, …

And in the labyrinth both son and sire
Awhile will fan and fuel hatred's fire;
Sparks of the log of evil are all men …

The way of vice is open as the sky,
The way of virtue's like the needle's eye;
But whether here or there, the eager Soul …

For this I say, Be watchful of the Cage
Of chance; it opes alike to fool and sage;
Spy on the moment, for to-morrow'll be, …

Flowers hast thou in thyself, and foliage,
And what is good, and what is glad to see;
The sun is not so bright as thy visàge; …

Beauty in woman; the high will's decree;
Fair knighthood arm'd for manly exercise;
The pleasant song of birds; love's soft replies; …

Dante, a sigh that rose from the heart's core
Assail'd me, while I slumber'd, suddenly;
So that I woke o' the instant, fearing sore …

Though thou, indeed, hast quite forgotten ruth,
Its steadfast truth my heart abandons not;
But still its thought yields service in good part …

Just look, Manetto, at that wry-mouth'd minx;
Merely take notice what a wretch it is;
How well contrived in her deformities, …

Lo! I am she who makes the wheel to turn;
Lo! I am she who gives and takes away;
Blamed idly, day by day, …

Faith, idol, Ka'ba-fane am I; who smites the sphere a-spin am I;
The cloud upon the heavens' face, likewise the rains that rain am I.
 …

Come and let us two be comrades, come and let us seek the Loved One;
Come and let us two be helpmates, come and let us seek the Loved One.
 …

Mirie it is while sumer ilast
  With fugheles song;
Oc nu necheth windes blast …

Hit wes upon a Scere Þorsday þat vre Louerd aros;
Ful milde were þe wordes He spec to Iudas:
 …

Wynter wakeneth al my care,
Nou this leves waxeth bare;
Ofte I sike ant mourne sare …

From the garden of Heaven a western breeze
Blows through the leaves of my garden of earth;
With a love like a huri I’ld take mine ease, …

Lay not reproach at the drunkard’s door
Oh Fanatic, thou that art pure of soul;
Not thine on the page of life to enrol …

Slaves of thy shining eyes are even those
That diadems of might and empire bear;
Drunk with the wine that from thy red lip flows, …

The rose is not fair without the beloved’s face,
Nor merry the Spring without the sweet laughter of wine;
The path through the fields, and winds from a flower-strewn place, …

Last night I dreamed that angels stood without
The tavern door, and knocked in vain, and wept;
They took the clay of Adam, and, methought, …

Cypress and Tulip and sweet Eglantine,
Of these the tale from lip to lip is sent;
Washed by three cups, oh Saki, of thy wine, …

True love has vanished from every heart;
What has befallen all lovers fair?
When did the bonds of friendship part?— …

Flee fro the prees, and dwelle with sothfastnesse,
Suffyce unto thy good, though hit be smal;
For hord hath hate, and climbing tikelnesse, …

A blisful lyf, a paisible and a swete
Ledden the peples in the former age;
They helde hem payed of fruites, that they ete, …

Madame, ye ben of al beautè shryne
As fer as cercled is the mappemounde;
For as the cristal glorious ye shyne, …

Loke wel aboute, ye that lovers be;
Lat nat your lustes lede you to dotage;
Be nat enamoured on al thing that ye see. …

Go forth, king, rule thee by sapience;
Bishop, be able to minister doctryne;
Lord, to trew consayl yeve audience; …

O mortal folk, you may behold and see
  How I lie here, sometime a mighty knight;
The end of joy and all prosperitee …

Thou Perfect Being, Source whence wisdom's mysteries arise;
Things, the issue of Thine essence, show wherein Thy nature lies.
 …

Stately Cypress yesterday her shade threw o'er my head;
Her form was heart-ensnaring, heart-delighting her light tread;
When speaking, sudden opened she her smiling rubies red, …

Yield not the soul to pang of Love, for Love's the soul's fierce glow;
That Love's the torment of the soul doth all the wide world know.
 …

'Tis grief and mourning Zeyd renewed always;
From his wailing ceased he not, he wept aye.
 …

Forget not yet the tried intent
Of such a truth as I have meant;
My great travail so gladly spent, …

Sing lullaby, as women do,
  Wherewith they bring their babes to rest;
And lullaby can I sing too, …

Hey! now the day dawis;
The jolly cock crawis;
Now shroudis the shawis …

A silly shepherd lately sat
Among a flock of sheep;
Where musing long on this and that, …

Foolish love is only folly;
Wanton love is too unholy;
Greedy love is covetous; …

The worldly prince doth in his sceptre hold
A kind of heaven in his authorities;
The wealthy miser, in his mass of gold, …

Passions are liken’d best to floods and streams:
  The shallow murmur, but the deep are dumb;
So, when affection yields discourse, it seems …

MOST glorious Lord of Lyfe! that, on this day,
Didst make Thy triumph over death and sin;
And, having harrowd hell, didst bring away …

Long-while I sought to what I might compare
Those powrefull eies which lighten my dark spright;
Yet find I nought on earth, to which I dare …

One day I sought with her hart-thrilling eies
To make a truce, and termes to entertaine;
All fearlesse then of so false enimies, …

Penelope, for her Ulisses sake,
Deviz’d a web her wooers to deceave;
In which the worke that she all day did make, …

Sweet is the rose, but growes upon a brere;
Sweet is the iunipeer; but sharpe his bough;
Sweet is the eglantine, but pricketh nere; …

Tell me, when shall these wearie woes have end;
Or shall their ruthlesse torment never cease,
But al my days in pining languor spend, …

Mark when she smiles with amiable cheare,
And tell me whereto can ye lyken it;
When on each eyelid sweetly doe appeare …

Shall I then silent be, or shall I speake?
And if I speake, her wrath renew I shall;
And if I silent be, my hart will breake, …

Trust not the treason of those smyling lookes,
Untill ye have their guylefull traynes well tryde;
For they are lyke but unto golden hookes, …

Fayre is my Love, when her fayre golden haires
With the loose wynd ye waving chance to marke;
Fayre, when the rose in her red cheekes appeares, …

Let not one sparke of filthy lustfull fyre
Breake out, that may her sacred peace molest;
Ne one light glance of sensuall desyre …

You sacred sea-nymphs pleasantly disporting
Amidst this wat'ry world, where now I sail;
If ever love, or lovers sad reporting, …

The dewy roseate Morn had with her hairs
In sundry sorts the Indian clime adorned;
And now her eyes apparrelèd in tears, …

Love guides the roses of thy lips,
And flies about them like a bee;
If I approach he forward skips, …

I wrote in Mirrha's bark, and as I wrote,
Poor Mirrha wept because I wrote forsaken;
'Twas of thy pride I sung in weeping note, …

I part; but how? from joy, from hope, from life;
I leave; but whom? love's pride, wit's pomp, heart's bliss;
I pine; for what? for grief, for thought, for strife; …

Some praise the looks, and others praise the locks
Of their fair queens, in love with curious words;
Some laud the breast where love his treasure locks, …

Now I find thy looks were feignèd,
Quickly lost, and quickly gainèd;
Soft thy skin, like wool of wethers, …

Weep not, my wanton, smile upon my knee;
When thou art old there’s grief enough for thee.
      Mother’s wag, pretty boy, …

The loppèd tree in time may grow again,
Most naked plants renew both fruit and flower;
The sorest wight may find release of pain, …

There is a garden in her face
  Where roses and white lilies blow;
A heavenly paradise is that place, …

  Rose-cheek’d _Laura_, come;
Sing thou smoothly with thy beauty’s
Silent music, either other …

Of Neptune’s empire let us sing,
At whose command the waves obey;
To whom the rivers tribute pay, …

Next morning, when the golden sunne was risen,
And new had bid good morrow to the mountaines;
When night her silver light had lockt in prison, …

Of all the kindes of common countrey life,
Methinkes a shepheards life is most content;
His state is quiet peace, devoyd of strife; …

Loe here behold these tributarie teares
Paid to thy faire but cruell tyrant eyes;
Loe here the blossome of my youthfull yeares, …

I ask but right, let her that caught me late,
    Either love, or cause that I may never hate;
    I crave too much--would she but let me love her; …

In summer's heat, and mid-time of the day,
    To rest my limbs upon a bed I lay;
    One window shut, the other open stood, …

All lovers war, and Cupid hath his tent;
    Attic, all lovers are to war far sent,
    What age fits Mars, with Venus doth agree; …

Leave colouring thy tresses, I did cry;
    Now hast thou left no hairs at all to dye.
    But what had been more fair had they been kept? …

I mean not to defend the scapes of any,
    Or justify my vices being many;
    For I confess, if that might merit favour, …

GrÊcinus (well I wot) thou told'st me once,
    I could not be in love with two at once;
    By thee deceived, by thee surprised am I, …

The lofty pine, from high Mount Pelion raught,
    Ill ways by rough seas wondering waves first taught;
    Which rashly 'twixt the sharp rocks in the deep, …

Fool, if to keep thy wife thou hast no need,
    Keep her from me, my more desire to breed;
    We scorn things lawful; stolen sweets we affect; …

I sit not here the noble horse to see;
    Yet whom thou favour'st, pray may conqueror be.
    To sit and talk with thee I hither came, …

If Thetis and the Morn their sons did wail,
    And envious Fates great goddesses assail;
    Sad Elegy, thy woful hairs unbind: …

Come were the times of Ceres' sacrifice;
    In empty bed alone my mistress lies.
    Golden-haired Ceres crowned with ears of corn, …

Long have I borne much, mad thy faults me make;
    Dishonest love, my wearied breast forsake!
    Now have I freed myself, and fled the chain, …

Look in thy glass and tell the face thou viewest
Now is the time that face should form another;
Whose fresh repair if now thou not renewest, …

As fast as thou shalt wane, so fast thou grow'st,
In one of thine, from that which thou departest;
And that fresh blood which youngly thou bestow'st, …

When I do count the clock that tells the time,
And see the brave day sunk in hideous night;
When I behold the violet past prime, …

Not from the stars do I my judgement pluck;
And yet methinks I have astronomy,
But not to tell of good or evil luck, …

Devouring Time, blunt thou the lion's paws,
And make the earth devour her own sweet brood;
Pluck the keen teeth from the fierce tiger's jaws, …

A woman's face with nature's own hand painted,
Hast thou, the master mistress of my passion;
A woman's gentle heart, but not acquainted …

My glass shall not persuade me I am old,
So long as youth and thou are of one date;
But when in thee time's furrows I behold, …

Mine eye hath play'd the painter and hath stell'd,
Thy beauty's form in table of my heart;
My body is the frame wherein 'tis held, …

Weary with toil, I haste me to my bed,
The dear respose for limbs with travel tir'd;
But then begins a journey in my head …

Thy bosom is endeared with all hearts,
Which I by lacking have supposed dead;
And there reigns Love, and all Love's loving parts, …

Take all my loves, my love, yea take them all;
What hast thou then more than thou hadst before?
No love, my love, that thou mayst true love call; …

That thou hast her it is not all my grief,
And yet it may be said I loved her dearly;
That she hath thee is of my wailing chief, …

When most I wink, then do mine eyes best see,
For all the day they view things unrespected;
But when I sleep, in dreams they look on thee, …

If the dull substance of my flesh were thought,
Injurious distance should not stop my way;
For then despite of space I would be brought, …

The other two, slight air, and purging fire
Are both with thee, wherever I abide;
The first my thought, the other my desire, …

Mine eye and heart are at a mortal war,
How to divide the conquest of thy sight;
Mine eye my heart thy picture's sight would bar, …

Not marble, nor the gilded monuments
Of princes, shall outlive this powerful rhyme;
But you shall shine more bright in these contents …

Like as the waves make towards the pebbled shore,
So do our minutes hasten to their end;
Each changing place with that which goes before, …

Sin of self-love possesseth all mine eye
And all my soul, and all my every part;
And for this sin there is no remedy, …

